## 2. Log Source Contract

The index naming is a **contract** — changing it breaks detection rules.

| Source | Index | Content | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| Authelia | `authelia-*` | Login attempts, MFA pass/fail, ACL denials | ✅ Ingested |
| Keycloak | `keycloak-*` | Token issuance, admin console access, session events | ✅ Ingested |
| Traefik | `traefik-*` | Every HTTP request, forward-auth result | ✅ Ingested |
| Coraza | `coraza-*` | WAF blocks, anomaly scores | ✅ Ingested |
| Zeek / Suricata | `filebeat-*` | Network layer | ✅ Ingested |
| **Active Directory (DC01)** | **`wazuh-*`** | **User/group changes, Kerberos, lockouts** | ❌ **Not ingested** |

The only remaining blind spot is **AD identity events**. Every other layer is
already flowing to Zone 4.

---

## 3. Why AD Events Matter

Without a Wazuh agent on DC01, the SOC cannot see:

- A user being added to `GRP_IT_Admin` → potential privilege escalation
- A group being modified → authorization model change
- A disabled account being re-enabled → revocation bypass
- Kerberos anomalies → Kerberoasting, pass-the-ticket
- Account lockouts → brute force attempts against the identity plane

For the Zero Trust story to be complete ("every identity decision reaches the
SOC"), this gap must be closed.

