## 4. Planned Deployment Steps

### 4.1 Zone 2 → Zone 4 reachability

DC01 must reach the Wazuh manager at `10.16.64.156:1514` (agent events) and
`:1515` (enrollment). Between the two networks there must be a route with an
explicit allow rule for both TCP ports.

**Verify from DC01 (PowerShell):**

```powershell
Test-NetConnection -ComputerName 10.16.64.156 -Port 1514
Test-NetConnection -ComputerName 10.16.64.156 -Port 1515
```

Both must return `TcpTestSucceeded : True`.

### 4.2 Install the Wazuh agent on DC01

```powershell
# Download the MSI from the Wazuh manager (hosted by the Wazuh API)
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/windows/wazuh-agent-4.x.x-1.msi" `
  -OutFile "$env:TEMP\wazuh-agent.msi"

# Install, pointing at the manager
msiexec.exe /i "$env:TEMP\wazuh-agent.msi" /q `
  WAZUH_MANAGER="10.16.64.156" `
  WAZUH_AGENT_NAME="CORP-DC01" `
  WAZUH_REGISTRATION_SERVER="10.16.64.156"
```

### 4.3 Configure event collection

Edit `C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\ossec.conf`:

```xml
<localfile>
  <location>Security</location>
  <log_format>eventchannel</log_format>
</localfile>
```

### 4.4 Key Event IDs to Capture

| Event ID | Meaning | Threat signal |
|---|---|---|
| `4720` | User account created | Rogue account creation |
| `4726` | User account deleted | Cover-up / cleanup |
| `4732` | Member added to security-enabled group | Privilege escalation |
| `4733` | Member removed from group | Authorization change |
| `4740` | Account locked out | Brute force |
| `4625` | Failed logon | Credential attack |

### 4.5 Start the agent

```powershell
Start-Service -Name WazuhSvc
Get-Service -Name WazuhSvc
```

### 4.6 Verify on the manager

```bash
# On Zone 4 (minisoc2)
/var/ossec/bin/agent_control -l
# Expected: ID 001, Name CORP-DC01, Status Active
```

### 4.7 Verify in Kibana

```
event.code: 4732
```

Should return hits when a group is modified in AD.

